> Patric Mueller <bhaak@bigfoot.com> wrote:
>> Jonadab the Unsightly One <jonadab.theunsightlyone@gmail.com> wrote:
>>>
>>> * Introduce guaranteed levelport traps on some of the special
>>>   levels (e.g., Orcus Town), so that players with teleport control
>>>   can conveniently make a couple of stash trips without traversing
>>>   every level twice per trip.
>> 
>> Ah, I see you have a similar idea.
>
> I'm not sure about this idea, as mentioned elsewhere in the thread.
> Comparing it to portals, the level teleporters would be usable
> only once and require teleport control for being useful; they'd
> also be in a fixed (but possibly varying between games) location.

The portal I had in mind would also be in a fixed location, not
working like the Eye.

Something akin to the landmine that you can set up yourself and then
have it being stationary.

Maybe you would need 2 portal items, so that you can only set up a
connection when you've got 2. Finding one at the quest home level and
another one somewhere else?

Make them wishable? For vanilla this could be abusable but any serious
fork does something about wishes and pudding farming anyway.

> Portals would have to consume *some* resources, and they would have
> to disappear or at least become unusable when you have the Amulet.

Yes, that's of course a given. If you can't teleport with the amulet
you should certainly not be able to level teleport via any means.

Something like the amulet dropping from your inventory when passing
through a portal, so they would still be useful for escaping.

> I'm not sure what kind of resource requirements would be best, though.
>
>>> * Allow wands of digging to work in Gehennom the same way as in the
>>>   rest of the dungeon, so that cutting paths is less tedious.
>>>   (Although, this could also make the ascension run easier.)
>> 
>> They don't?
>
> As far as I know, they only dig one sqare in Gehennom.

I looked at the code and yes, when you are on a level with a maze flag
(so not only real maze levels), wands and spells of digging only work
for the first wall segment they encounter.

This behavior was added in 3.0.0, although there is no comment
explaining why.

Looking from our current POV, it seems uncredibly obnoxious to make
mazes - of which there are way too many - even more boring than they
already are. But I also find it interesting that I've never heard a
complain about that before.

